p.src:
In any orthonormal basis $\{\ket{i}\}$, $\operatorname{Tr}X=\sum_i\bra{i}X\ket{i}.$ The numerical value does not depend on the basis. For a pure state, $\begin{aligned}
\overline{M}
&=\operatorname{Tr}\!\left(\ket{\psi}\bra{\psi}M\right) \\
&=\sum_i\braket{i}{\psi}\bra{\psi}M\ket{i} \\
&=\bra{\psi}M\left(\sum_i\ket{i}\bra{i}\right)\ket{\psi} \\
&=\bra{\psi}M\ket{\psi}.\end{aligned}$ The resolution of the identity, $\sum_i\ket{i}\bra{i}=I,$ turns the density-matrix rule back into the familiar pure-state expectation value.

